<BaseLayout title="Classes: privacy and classroom rules — Biochemistrypedia" description="What teacher classes store, who can see it, how long it is kept, and the rules teachers agree to.">
  <article class="wrap tc">
    <p class="tc-eyebrow"><a href="/teach/">Classes</a> · Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy and classroom rules</h1>
    <p class="tc-lede">Classes are built to hold as little as possible about students. Here is all of it.</p>

    <h2>What we store</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Teachers:</strong> an email address (to sign in), a display name, and a password stored as a one-way hash.</li>
      <li><strong>Students:</strong> a nickname and a student code stored as a one-way hash. No email, no real name, no password, no age, no school.</li>
      <li><strong>Answers</strong> in lessons, for students in a class: the first try on each quiz question, the first agree or disagree pick on each opening statement, written answers the student chose to send, "done" marks and stuck notes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To slow down password guessing, sign-in and join attempts are counted per connection using a one-way hash of the IP address. Those counters are deleted within a day. The site counts page visits with Plausible, which sets no cookies.</p>

    <h2>Who can see it</h2>
    <p>Only the class's teacher sees student nicknames and answers. Room view shows answers without names. Nothing is sold, shared or used for advertising, and no AI model reads student work.</p>
    <p><strong>Groups</strong> (a school or department): the group's public page lists its name, description and teachers' display names, never emails or students. Teachers in a group see each other's names. Its admins also see members' emails, and each class's name, teacher and counts. Admins can read a class's student answers only if its teacher turns on "share this class's brief with the group". Admins can never change a class.</p>

    <h2>How long it's kept</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A teacher can remove a student, delete a class, or delete their whole account at any time; it is deleted at once.</li>
      <li>A class with no activity for about 13 months is deleted automatically.</li>
      <li>A student who "leaves" a class on a device signs that device out; their answers stay with the teacher until the teacher removes them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Rules for teachers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ask students to use a nickname or first name, never a full name or anything else that identifies them.</li>
      <li>Follow your school's or district's rules for online tools. If your district requires approval or a data agreement, get it first.</li>
      <li>For students under 13, make the roster yourself (Students tab) rather than letting them sign up, and follow your school's guidance on parental notice.</li>
      <li>Use classes for teaching. Don't use nicknames or answers to contact students outside class.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions or problems</h2>
    <p><a href="/contact">Get in touch</a>. If you think a class is being misused, tell us and we'll look at it.</p>
    <p class="tc-muted">First edition, October 2026. This page changes if the features change.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
